title: Proposition 73
---
order: 73
---
short_label: X.73
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If from a rational straight line there is subtracted a rational straight line commensurable with the whole in square only, then the remainder is irrational; let it be called an <i>apotome.</i>
----
proof:

From the rational straight line *AB* let the rational straight line *BC*, commensurable with the whole in square only, be subtracted.

I say that the remainder *AC* is the irrational straight line called apotome.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="65" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.73 diagram" src="propX73.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "X.73",
        elements: [
            "A0;point;free;-1000,35",
            "A3;point;free;1000,35",
            "A;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,30,0",
            "B;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,210,0",
            "C;point;lineSlider;A0,A3,100,0",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Since *AB* is incommensurable in length with *BC*, and *AB* is to *BC* as the square on *AB* is to the rectangle *AB* by *BC*, therefore the square on *AB* is incommensurable with the rectangle *AB* by *BC*. [!just X.11]

But the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC* is commensurable with the square on *AB*, and twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC* is commensurable with the rectangle *AB* by *BC*. [!just X.15; X.6]

And, inasmuch as the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC* equal twice the rectangle *AB* by *BC* together with the square on *CA*, therefore the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC* is also incommensurable with the remainder, the square on *AC*. [!just II.7, X.13; X.16]

But the sum of the squares on *AB* and *BC* is rational, therefore *AC* is irrational. Let it be called an apotome. [!just X.Def.I.4]

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide: This proposition is used very frequently in the rest of Book X starting with @X.75. It is also used in propositions @XIII.6 and @XIII.11.
